Use cRDCComboForm.pkg

/*
    Class:      cFTComboForm
    Purpose:    A cRDCComboForm that REFUSES the dialog's dark colouring, because Windows will not
                carry it into the drop-down list.

    THE PROBLEM (Nils 2026-08-22, Program Setup): "when the dark theme is applied the drop-down list
    for combos becomes hard to read - the not selected items have black text on a dark background".

    He is right, and it cannot be fixed by setting the colours harder. An FT dialog paints its native
    children with

        Broadcast Recursive Set Color     to (FTWebBg())
        Broadcast Recursive Set TextColor to (FTThemeInk())

    and that reaches the combo's EDIT portion. The drop-down is a separate window that Windows paints
    itself: it picks up the dark background but leaves unselected items at the system text colour,
    which is black. DataFlex offers no hook for it - DFBaseComboBox (Windows.pkg:6302) has no
    list-colour property, and the framework does not forward WM_CTLCOLORLISTBOX. The only way to
    colour those items would be to owner-draw the control and paint every row by hand.

    THE TRADE, AND IT IS NILS'S CALL: the combo stays in system colours - light - among dark
    controls. That is visibly inconsistent, and it is the better half of the bargain, because the
    alternative is a list nobody can read. A control that looks slightly out of place still works; a
    dark list with black text does not.

    HOW IT WORKS: the two Set messages are swallowed. The parent's Broadcast Recursive reaches every
    child AFTER each child's own End_Construct_Object, so a combo cannot simply re-assert its colours
    at construct time - whatever it set would be overwritten a moment later. Refusing the message is
    what survives the broadcast.

    Only ProgramSetupFT uses this. The non-FT ProgramSetup.dg keeps plain cRDCComboForm: that dialog
    is light, so its list was always readable and there is nothing to guard against.
*/

Class cFTComboForm is a cRDCComboForm

    /* ALL FIVE of this class's instances set exactly these four, identically - the strongest
       case in the app for a class default. A combo that wants a different size or a
       left-justified label still says so and wins; nothing else has to say anything.
    */
    Procedure Construct_Object
        Forward Send Construct_Object

        Set Size to 13 100
        Set Entry_State to False
        Set Label_Col_Offset to 2
        Set Label_Justification_Mode to JMode_Right
    End_Procedure

    // Swallowed on purpose - see the note above. Not "not implemented": deliberately inert, so the
    // dialog's Broadcast Recursive cannot drag this control into a colour scheme Windows will only
    // half-apply.
    Procedure Set Color Integer iColor
    End_Procedure

    Procedure Set TextColor Integer iColor
    End_Procedure

End_Class
